-- title:
--   Good-reduction Néron identity component of J₀(p) from the Deligne–Rapoport model
-- statement:
--   Let $p \ge 5$ be a prime, let $\mathfrak X$ be a `DRModelPackage p` — a package of data for the two-chart integral model `DRModel p` of the full modular function field of level $p$ with its Igusa $j$, over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$, including proper flat structure morphism, normality, curve models $M_0$ over $\mathbf Q$ and $M_\eta$ over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ identified with the corresponding fibres by isomorphisms $e_0, e_\eta$, the Galois compatibility of places, two sections $\varepsilon_\infty, \varepsilon_0$ and a maximal smooth locus — and let $I$ be a `LegTwoInputV2` for $\mathfrak X$. Assume the open subscheme of $M_\eta.C$ obtained as the preimage, under $e_\eta$ followed by the projection to `DRModel p`, of the image of the finite chart is nonempty, and that for each element $a$ of the finite chart algebra $\operatorname{chartAlgFin}$ over $\mathbf Z$ the germ of $a$ on that open subscheme, read in $M_\eta$'s function field and transported by $\mathfrak X.M_\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$ into `modularFunctionFieldBar p`, is the Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ obtained from the $q$-expansion of $a$ over $\mathbf Q$ by `coeffEmb`. Let $D$ be a `RelativePic0Designation` over $\mathbf Z$ for `DRModel.toBase p`, that is, a scheme with a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$ and a zero section splitting it, and let $hD$ witness that $D$ represents the subfunctor of the relative Picard functor of (`DRModel.toBase p`, $\mathfrak X.\varepsilon_\infty$) cut out by fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero: a rigidified line bundle on the pullback along $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ whose restriction to every geometric fibre is algebraically equivalent to zero, universal for rigidified bundles with that property, and trivial along the zero section. Assume finally that $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is smooth, separated, quasi-compact, surjective and `GeometricallyConnected`. Then `JZeroNeronIdentityComponentGood p` is nonempty: there is a smooth, separated, quasi-compact, surjective group scheme $g : G \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$ locally of finite type with commutative relative group law $L$ and preconnected fibres, a bijection of `JZero p` with its $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-points that is additive, Galois-equivariant and compatible with Hecke operators realised as endomorphisms, with $[n]$ flat and surjective for $n > 0$, the subgroup generated by $\mathbf Z$-sections of finite index in the Galois-fixed points, and with the further clauses of `JZeroNeronIdentityComponent p`, together with, for every prime $\ell$ not dividing $p$, good-prime data over the localisation $\mathbf Z_{(\ell)}$: a compatible group law and point parametrisation after base change, an abelian-scheme property bundle, a reduction dictionary for valuation subrings of $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ lying over $\ell$, and Hecke compatibility.
--
--   This is the construction of the Néron identity component of $J_0(p)$ over $\mathbf Z$, in the form of the good-reduction datum used later in the level-lowering argument: the relative $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of the Deligne–Rapoport model, once represented, supplies the group scheme together with its $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-points, Galois and Hecke structure, and good reduction at every prime away from $p$. It is the step invoked by [`ModularCurve.nonempty_jZeroNeronIdentityComponentGood`](thm.html#ModularCurve.nonempty_jZeroNeronIdentityComponentGood), where the hypotheses on $\mathfrak X$, $I$ and $D$ are discharged.
